{{Politics of Balisca}}
'''Anarchism in Balisca''' traces its roots to [[colonization of Balisca|colonial Balisca]] but was formed as a political movement during the 19th century. During the epoch of [[Immigration to Balisca|mass migrations]] of [[Illypno-Fosia|Illypno-Fosian]] and [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] labourers at the end of the nineteenth and the beginning of the twentieth century, [[w:Anarchism|anarchist ideas]] started to spread, particularly amongst the country’s {{W|labour movement}}. Along with the labour migrants, many Zong, Volisanian, Darasian and Breislandic political {{W|exile}}s arrived, many holding anarchist or [[w:Anarcho-syndicalism|anarcho-syndicalist]] ideas. Some did not come as exiles but rather as a type of political entrepreneur, including [[w:Giovanni Rossi (anarchist)|Giovanni Rossi]]'s anarchist [[w:Commune (intentional community)|commune]], and [[Alquería del Amor]], which lasted two decades and at one point consisted of 200 individuals. 

There are numerous variants of anarchism in Balisca, namely {{W|expropriative anarchism}} in the period leading up to the [[Baliscan Revolution]], the peasant anarchism in the countryside of [[Bayara]]; urban {{W|anarcho-syndicalism}} in [[Cerdanya]], Balisca's capital [[Castejón]]; and what is sometimes called "pure" anarchism in other cities such as [[Alessandria]]. However, these were complementary trajectories and had many {{W|ideological}} similarities. Early on, the success of the anarchist movement was sporadic. Anarchists would organize a [[w:Strike action|strike]] and ranks would swell. Usually, repression by police reduced the numbers again, but at the same time further [[w:Radicalization|radicalized]] many strikers. This cycle helped lead to an era of mutual violence towards the end of the 19th century in which armed anarchists and {{lang|es|pistoleros}}, armed men paid by company owners, were both responsible for political assassinations, eventually sparking the [[Cantonal War]].

In the 20th century, this violence began to fade, and the movement gained speed with the rise of anarcho-syndicalism and the creation of the huge {{W|libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist}} {{W|trade union}}, the [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]] (CAT). {{W|General strike}}s became common, and large portions of the Baliscan working class adopted anarchist ideas. There also emerged a small {{W|individualist anarchist}} movement based on publications such as {{lang|es|[[Iniciales]]}} and {{lang|es|[[Libertad]]}}. The {{lang|bl|italics=no|[[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]]}} (FAT) was created as a purely anarchist association, with the intention of maintaining labor unions focused on the principles of anarchism. Anarchists played a central role in the fight for the {{W|cantonalism|cantonalist}} movement against the government of [[Josèp Bernat]]. 

Later, in the aftermath of the [[Red Summer]], a far-reaching [[Baliscan Revolution|social revolution]] swept the country. The revolution resulted in the abolition of the Baliscan republican system and the widespread implementation of anarchist and more specifically [[w:libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] principles throughout various portions of society, including the {{W|free migration|abolition of internal borders}}, {{W|secularization}}, and {{W|democratic socialism}}. Much of the [[economy of Balisca]] was put under {{W|worker control}}, while {{W|social welfare}} was significantly expanded through the {{W|redistribution of wealth|redistribution of income and wealth}}. Worker committees gained control of their factories, [[w:agriculture|agrarian]] areas became collectivized and run as libertarian socialist {{W|agricultural commune}}s.

The mass empowerment and embrace of various groups in this bottom-up system has been described as a "{{W|people's commune}}" or an "[[w:anarchism|anarchist]] {{W|republic}}", one based on the theories of {{W|anarcho-communism}} and built on a network of [[trade unions in Balisca|trade union]]s, {{W|factory committee}}s and [[w:Popular assembly|popular assemblies]]. The assemblies were used by the local citizens as a form of {{W|referendum}}s, where decisions were made through {{W|direct democracy}}, and became the basis for [[w:Civil law (common law)|civil law]]. The vast majority of Balisca's political decisions were made independently of {{W|Central Authority}}, through a system of {{W|libertarian municipalism|radical libertarian}} {{W|self-governance}} at the [[communes of Balisca|communal]] and [[cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] levels. Networks of "[[W:Soviet democracy|free councils]]" acted as institutions of {{W|participatory democracy}}, where issues would be discussed and dealt with directly.

Presently, anarchism in Balisca has evolved into the [[autonomous commune (Balisca)|autonomous communes]], which are neighborhoods or districts throughout the country that have elected to [[local secession (Balisca)|secede from the authority]] of their local government and establish themselves as {{W|Temporary Autonomous Zone|permanent autonomous zones}}. With the advent of {{W|social media}}, {{W|e-democracy}}, {{W|open-source governance|open-politics}} and the widespread {{W|technological literacy}} of the Baliscan population, it is stated by supporters that the autonomous communes are the next step in the evolution of {{w|direct democracy}}, and demonstrate that the {{W|anti-statism|Baliscan state is becoming increasingly redundant}}. 

